<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Further Austria</h1><p class="st-meta">1379 CE – 1805 CE (approx.) · capital Freiburg im Breisgau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Habsburgs' scattered western lands in Swabia, Alsace and the Breisgau, governed for four centuries from Ensisheim and Freiburg.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> came from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">County of Habsburg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a> and held scattered lands across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia-bavaria">Swabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-neuberg">Treaty of Neuberg</a> of 1379 divided the family's possessions, giving the western territories, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/inner-austria">Inner Austria</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-duke-of-austria">Leopold III</a>; these western lands became known as Further <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Further <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was a patchwork: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breisgau">Breisgau</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundgau">Sundgau</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, the forest towns on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, the margraviate of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-burgau">Burgau</a> and the county of Hohenberg. It was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ensisheim">Ensisheim</a> and later Freiburg, often together with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tyrol">County of Tyrol</a>, and had its own estates. Heavy losses to the Swiss followed in the 15th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-freiburg">University of Freiburg</a> was founded in 1457.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> gave the Alsatian lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1648. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-pressburg-1805">Peace of Pressburg</a> in 1805 the remaining territories were divided among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-baden">Grand Duchy of Baden</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a>, ending <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> rule in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/further-austria.jpg" alt="Further Austria" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: SpinnerLaserzthe2nd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Austria_(1230%E2%80%931934,_1945%E2%80%932000).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1379 CE – 1805 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Freiburg im Breisgau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Leopold III, Sigismund of Tyrol</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a>, <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Anterior Austria, Vorderösterreich</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1592&amp;focus=further-austri